// #taxon[Taxon]
#title[Equality and Identity types]

A distinguishing feature of MLTT is the introduction of intentional equality in
addition to the strict syntactic definitional equality. This way we can use
MLTT to talk about equality just like other propositions that we model using
types.

The formation rule for the family of identity types states that given any two
terms $a,b:A$ we may form the type of identifications between them.
$
  "Formation"
  (Gamma tack a:A)/
  (Gamma, x:A tack "Id"_A (a,x) "type")
$

The introduction rule essentially states that things that are judgementally
equal should have a canonical witness of equality inhabiting the identity type.
We call this witness $"refl"$. In order words, equality should be _reflexive_.
$
  "Introduction"
  (Gamma tack a:A)/
  (Gamma tack "refl"_a : "Id"_A (a,a))
$


